  "hindi": "इसलिए एक सौ बीस अध्यायों का प्रत्येक पद, प्रत्येक पाद और प्रत्येक श्लोक करके वर्णन किया जाना और सुना जाना चाहिए। क्यों? क्योंकि द्रव्य, रस, गुण, वीर्य, विपाक, दोष, धातु, मल, आशय, मर्म, सिरा, स्नायु, सन्धि, अस्थि, गर्भ की उत्पत्ति और द्रव्यों के समूहों के विभाग सूक्ष्म हैं; तथा खोए हुए शल्य को निकालना, व्रण का निश्चय और भग्न के भेद सूक्ष्म हैं; रोगों की साध्यता, याप्यता और प्रत्याख्येयता भी सूक्ष्म है; और इसी प्रकार के अन्य हज़ारों विशेष विषय हैं, जो विचार करने पर निर्मल और विशाल बुद्धि वाले की भी बुद्धि को व्याकुल कर दें, तो अल्प बुद्धि वाले का तो कहना ही क्या! इसलिए अवश्य ही प्रत्येक पद, प्रत्येक पाद और प्रत्येक श्लोक करके उनका वर्णन किया जाना और सुना जाना चाहिए।",
  "english": "Therefore the hundred and twenty chapters should be recounted and listened to word by word, quarter-verse by quarter-verse and verse by verse. Why? Because subtle are the substances (dravya), tastes (rasa), qualities (guṇa), potencies (vīrya), post-digestive changes (vipāka), doṣas, dhātus, wastes, receptacles, vital spots, vessels, sinews, joints, bones, the origin of the embryo, and the divisions of groups of substances; likewise the extraction of lost foreign bodies, the ascertainment of wounds, and the varieties of fractures; and the curable, manageable and to-be-declined nature of disorders; and thousands of other such particulars, which, when pondered, would confuse the mind even of one of clear and vast intellect, how much more that of one of little intellect. Therefore they must necessarily be recounted and listened to word by word, quarter-verse by quarter-verse and verse by verse.",
  "text_flags": [],
  "notes": "Very long compound; the grouping of members is a judgement. (1) dravya ... garbhasaṃbhava is read as one dvandva list, with dravyasamūha-vibhāgāḥ ('divisions of groups of substances') as the final member; the repeated dravya could also mean the list is two parallel sets. (2) pranaṣṭaśalyoddharaṇa-vraṇaviniścaya-bhagnavikalpāḥ: vikalpāḥ may govern only 'bhagna' (varieties of fracture) or all three members; the first reading is used. (3) anupada-pāda-śloka-m is read as 'word by word, quarter-verse by quarter-verse, verse by verse' (adverbial, acc.); it could also be read as 'each word, each quarter-verse, each verse'. (4) saviṃśam adhyāyaśatam = a hundred chapters plus twenty = 120, as the text says. (5) 'sūkṣmāḥ' agrees with the plural vibhāgāḥ, vikalpāḥ, viśeṣāḥ; sādhyayāpyapratyākhyeyatā is singular, so 'subtle' is applied to it loosely in the translation. The technical terms are glossed by their plain sense only.",
